I think this is the most important milestone in Chapter 2 because it introduces runtime messaging, which is the backbone of your Chrome extension. Every future feature—AI chat, GitHub integration, MCP, PDF chat, and OCR—will use this communication pattern.

For this milestone, we'll not connect to the backend yet. Instead, we'll verify that the communication between the popup and the background service worker works correctly.

Milestone 4 – Background Service Worker
Objective

Build the Background Service Worker that:

Receives messages from the popup
Logs the request
Processes the request (mock)
Returns a response
Verifies runtime messaging

At the end of this milestone, the flow will be:

Popup
   │
   │ sendMessage()
   ▼
Background Service Worker
   │
   │ Process Request
   ▼
Return Mock Response
   │
   ▼
Popup Updates UI
Step 1 – Background Architecture

Chrome extensions consist of several isolated environments.

                Chrome Extension

        ┌──────────────────────────┐
        │                          │
        │        Popup UI          │
        │        (React)           │
        └────────────┬─────────────┘
                     │
                     │ Runtime Messaging
                     ▼
        ┌──────────────────────────┐
        │ Background ServiceWorker │
        └────────────┬─────────────┘
                     │
                     │ (Future)
                     ▼
             Node.js Backend API

The popup cannot stay alive once it is closed.

The Background Service Worker is responsible for:

API Communication
Authentication
Notifications
Context Menu
Long Running Tasks
Message Routing
